DP  - 2026 Jul 20
TI  - Magnetic fluorescent probe-integrated flow cytometry for rapid and sensitive 
      immunodetection of enrofloxacin residues in aquatic products.
LID - 10.1039/d6ra04389e [doi]
AB  - Enrofloxacin, the first fluoroquinolone antibiotic, has been widely applied in 
      aquaculture. Long-term dietary intake of enrofloxacin residues may cause allergic 
      reactions and toxic damage in humans. Hence, it is imperative to develop a rapid 
      detection method for enrofloxacin residues in complex aquatic products. Herein, a 
      magnetic fluorescent probe (MFP) was synthesized and coupled with flow cytometry 
      (FC) to establish an indirect competitive immunoassay for the determination of 
      enrofloxacin. The developed MFP-FC method has the advantages of simple 
      pretreatment and high sensitivity. While the final FC instrumental measurement 
      only requires 1 min, the testing procedure involves a 35 min sample preparation 
      step as well as two separate 120 min incubation periods. Under optimal 
      conditions, the detection process can be finished within 1 min. The limit of 
      detection (LOD) was 1.00 ng mL(-1), and the working dynamic range of this assay 
      was 3.50-47.07 ng mL(-1). Furthermore, the proposed method has been successfully 
      applied to the detection of enrofloxacin in aquatic products (American bullfrog, 
      small yellow croaker and crucian carp), with acceptable recoveries ranging from 
      77.9% to 116.0%.
