TI  - A decision-making process to guide the potential application of a hepatitis A 
      virus and parvovirus B19 nucleic acid test for qualifying plasma for 
      fractionation.
PG  - 155-164
LID - 10.3138/jammi-2025-0013 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The Roche cobas DPX assay (Roche Diagnostics) is a nucleic acid test 
      (NAT) that detects parvovirus B19 (B19V) and hepatitis A virus (HAV) and enhances 
      the safety of fractionated plasma protein products. Implementation of this NAT 
      requires multiple decisions focused on test characteristics, test 
      operationalization, and result interpretation. This study uses a decision-making 
      process to guide the potential application of this NAT to qualify plasma for 
      fractionation. METHODS: A basic decision tree approach was used to undertake a 
      stepwise feasibility study of the B19V/HAV assay in a blood donor setting. Limit 
      of detection, reproducibility, and pooling studies utilized mocked-up B19V 
      (genotypes 1 and 2) and HAV (genotypes 1a, 2a, and 3a) specimens (BioQControl; 
      Biologicals Quality Control B.V.), Somagen (LGC SERACARE), and anonymized 
      COVID-19 convalescent plasma (CCP; Canadian Blood Services). The Hamilton 
      Microlab STAR IVD Pipettor generated HAV and B19V specimen pools of 96, 24, and 
      6. Pools and individual specimens were tested using the DPX (Duplex HAV and 
      Parvovirus B19 Nucleic Acid Test) on the cobas 6800/8800 System. Data were 
      collated using Excel (Microsoft, Seattle, WA, USA). RESULTS: The Roche cobas DPX 
      reproducibly detected dilutions of B19V and HAV. Pools of 24 and 6 reproducibly 
      detected B19V at acceptable Ct values; pools of 6 reproducibly detected HAV, 
      although at later Ct values. CONCLUSIONS: Using a simple decision tree approach, 
      this study describes how a B19V and HAV NAT could be applied for the 
      qualification of plasma for fractionation.
