TI  - Noninvasive SERS Immunosensing of Tyrosinase for Melanoma Monitoring via 
      Microneedle Sampling Integrated with Satellite-Structured Bifunctional Nanozymes.
PG  - 20272-20283
LID - 10.1021/acs.analchem.6c01543 [doi]
AB  - The noninvasive and in situ monitoring of melanoma biomarkers remains a 
      significant challenge in clinical diagnostics. This study reports a novel sensing 
      platform that integrates a wearable microneedle (MN) patch with 
      satellite-structured CoFe(2)O(4)-Au@Pt nanoparticles (NPs) featuring both highly 
      efficient mimic enzyme and surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) activity for 
      detection of tyrosinase, aiming at melanoma monitoring. First, the MN patch 
      facilitates the sampling of cutaneous interstitial fluid and the immunocapture of 
      tyrosinase. Then, the captured tyrosinase is quantified using a SERS immunoassay, 
      where the CoFe(2)O(4)-Au@Pt NPs serve as bifunctional materials of signal 
      transducers and powerful SERS substrates. Their intrinsic oxidase-mimicking 
      activity catalyzes the oxidation of 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), while 
      their superior SERS activity enables the highly sensitive readout of the oxidized 
      TMB (oxTMB) product. The developed detection system demonstrates the capability 
      to quantify TYR levels in skin models over a linear range of 0.01 ng/mL to 10 
      mug/mL, with a limit of detection (LOD) as low as 6.61 pg/mL. Notably, the system 
      exhibits robust selectivity and strong anti-interference capacity against complex 
      biological matrices. Using a preclinical B16F10 murine melanoma model, the 
      platform successfully achieves the quantification of tyrosinase levels, 
      demonstrating its capability to provide high-fidelity information for melanoma 
      prognosis and longitudinal disease monitoring. Thus, the integration of MN-based 
      sampling, nanozyme-mediated amplification, and in situ SERS detection paves the 
      way for novel diagnostic approaches in melanoma management.
