LA  - eng
PT  - Journal Article
DEP - 20260625
PL  - England
TA  - Bioanalysis
JT  - Bioanalysis
JID - 101512484
SB  - IM
OAB - What is the problem?Rituximab is a medicine commonly used to treat a kidney 
      disease called membranous nephropathy. However, some patients do not respond well 
      to this treatment. One possible reason is that the patient's immune system 
      produces antibodies that attack the medicine itself, called anti-rituximab 
      antibodies. Detecting these antibodies accurately, especially when they are 
      present at very low levels, is challenging with current standard tests.What did 
      we do?We created a new, highly sensitive laboratory test to detect these 
      anti-rituximab antibodies in blood samples. Our new method, called a 
      time-resolved fluorescence immunoassay (TRFIA), uses a special fluorescent label 
      that makes the signal much brighter and easier to measure, reducing background 
      noise. We carefully checked that our new test is reliable, accurate, and 
      specific. We also compared it to a commonly used commercial test kit (ELISA).What 
      did we find?Our new test proved to be more sensitive than the standard test, 
      meaning it can detect smaller amounts of the antibodies. It also performed very 
      consistently. When we used our new test on blood samples from 55 patients whose 
      kidney disease did not improve with rituximab treatment, we found that about 38% 
      of them had these anti-rituximab antibodies in their blood. The amounts of 
      antibodies varied greatly between patients. Interestingly, the two patients with 
      the very highest antibody levels had previously experienced allergic-type 
      reactions to the rituximab infusion.Why does it matter?This new, more sensitive 
      test can help doctors identify patients who are not responding to rituximab 
      because their body is producing antibodies against it. Knowing this early can 
      guide treatment decisions, such as considering a switch to a different, 
      potentially more effective medication for those patients. Our work provides a 
      better tool to understand and manage treatment resistance in this kidney disease.
