TI  - Conformational ensemble-guided hapten design for improved immunorecognition of 
      phenylbutazone in herbal tea by lateral flow immunoassay.
PG  - 130152
LID - S0039-9140(26)00808-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.talanta.2026.130152 [doi]
AB  - Phenylbutazone is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) prohibited in 
      food-producing animals due to its potential risks to food safety, requiring rapid 
      and reliable detection methods. In this study, four haptens derived from 
      phenylbutazone were rationally designed and evaluated for antibody production and 
      lateral flow immunoassay (LFIA) development. In addition to conventional static 
      similarity analysis, a conformational ensemble-based approach was introduced to 
      better capture molecular flexibility. Although the haptens exhibited comparable 
      structural similarity and physicochemical properties, significant differences in 
      immunoreactivity were observed. Further recognition-region analysis suggested 
      that linker design mainly modulated whole-molecule conformational diversity and 
      epitope exposure without markedly disrupting the conserved phenylbutazone-like 
      scaffold. Hapten C showed the best overall immunoreactivity and was therefore 
      selected for antibody production and gold nanoparticle-based LFIA (GNPs-LFIA) 
      development, achieving a cut-off value of 100 ng/mL, a limit of detection (LOD) 
      of 4.29 ng/mL, and a working range of 5-80 ng/mL. The assay showed high 
      specificity, satisfactory accuracy, and good agreement with high-performance 
      liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (HPLC-MS/MS) in real sample 
      analysis. This study provides new insights into hapten design and offers a rapid 
      method for phenylbutazone detection in complex matrices.
