TI  - Luminescence-guided engineering of anti-idiotype nanobody-NanoLuc fusion reagents 
      for sensitive monitoring of free therapeutic antibodies.
PG  - 130112
LID - S0039-9140(26)00768-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.talanta.2026.130112 [doi]
AB  - Therapeutic antibody monitoring requires bioanalytical platforms combining high 
      sensitivity, specificity, and robustness in complex biological matrices. Here, we 
      report the development of a sensitive bioluminescent bridging immunoassay for 
      detecting free, paratope-accessible therapeutic antibodies using anti-idiotype 
      nanobody-NanoLuc fusion reagents. To streamline reagent generation, we 
      implemented a luminescence-guided workflow integrating camelid immunization with 
      a therapeutic monoclonal antibody cocktail, competitive phage display enrichment 
      for paratope-directed binders against adalimumab, and direct screening of 
      enriched pools at the nanobody-NanoLuc fusion level in Escherichia coli. This 
      approach enabled early identification of highly active chimeras while preserving 
      functional specificity, overcoming common nanobody-dependent expression 
      bottlenecks during assay development. Selected binders were incorporated into a 
      bridging assay format using a biotinylated nanobody as capture reagent and a 
      nanobody-NanoLuc chimera for signal generation. The resulting assay showed a 
      linear response over the 0-50 ng/mL range (R(2) = 0.998), with a limit of 
      detection of 0.22 ng/mL. Recovery studies performed in adalimumab-spiked human 
      serum within the clinically relevant therapeutic range demonstrated high accuracy 
      and precision. Application to a limited panel of clinically stratified patient 
      samples yielded measurements consistent with the expected treatment response 
      status. These results support a modular bioanalytical workflow for free 
      therapeutic antibody monitoring and the broader selection of functional 
      Nb-NanoLuc reagents for immunoassay applications.
