TI  - Fabrication of a robust and ultra-sensitive SERS platform via photoaffinity 
      labeling-mediated oriented and covalent antibody immobilization for aflatoxin 
      B(1) biosensing.
PG  - 118886
LID - S0956-5663(26)00518-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2026.118886 [doi]
AB  - To address the critical need for robust and ultrasensitive detection of aflatoxin 
      B(1) (AFB(1)), we developed a surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) 
      immunosensor featuring a precise, covalent, and uniformly oriented bio-interface. 
      This was achieved via a genetically engineered photoaffinity labeling strategy. 
      Specifically, an engineered Fc-binding protein-incorporating a photoreactive 
      unnatural amino acid and a terminal cysteine tag-was utilized to 
      site-specifically label the antibody Fc region under mild UV irradiation. The 
      resulting thiol-modified antibody was then directionally anchored onto a gold 
      nanoparticle substrate via robust Au-S self-assembly, ensuring an optimal 
      "Fab-up" configuration. Relying on this highly efficient architecture, an 
      indirect competitive SERS immunoassay was constructed. The platform exhibited an 
      exceptional limit of detection (LOD) of 0.2 pg mL(-1) and a broad linear dynamic 
      range spanning from 0.001 to 10 ng mL(-1), alongside excellent specificity 
      against co-occurring mycotoxins. Applied to real peanut oil extracts, the assay 
      demonstrated highly reliable recoveries (99.4%-106.8%, RSD <6%) that perfectly 
      correlated with standard LC-MS/MS validation. This work provides a highly viable 
      analytical tool for trace food safety surveillance and establishes a universal 
      paradigm for precision-engineering high-performance optical biosensing 
      interfaces.
