TI  - Piezoelectric sensors in point-of-care testing.
PG  - 127-160
LID - S0065-2423(26)00019-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/bs.acc.2026.01.005 [doi]
AB  - This paper presents a comprehensive overview of piezoelectric (PE) biosensors and 
      chemosensors tailored for point-of-care testing (POCT) applications. Emphasis was 
      placed on the integration of PE transducers-such as quartz crystal microbalances 
      (QCM), bulk acoustic wave (BAW) devices, and cantilevers-with biological and 
      synthetic recognition elements to enable sensitive, rapid, and decentralized 
      diagnostics. Selected studies were critically evaluated based on their analytical 
      performance, including limit of detection, dynamic range, specificity, and 
      operational stability. Notably, the incorporation of nanomaterials and engineered 
      PE compounds significantly enhanced signal transduction and assay sensitivity. 
      The versatility of PE platforms in detecting diverse analytes-from infectious 
      disease markers to cancer biomarkers-demonstrates their potential for widespread 
      clinical deployment. The survey underscores the technological maturity and 
      adaptability of PE POCT systems, positioning them as viable alternatives to 
      conventional optical and electrochemical sensors.
