TI  - Engineered rabbit monoclonal antibodies and core-gap-shell gold nanoparticles for 
      dual-modal lateral flow detection of vitamin B(2) in serum.
PG  - 118822
LID - S0956-5663(26)00454-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2026.118822 [doi]
AB  - Vitamin B(2) (VB(2), riboflavin), an essential water-soluble vitamin, plays a 
      critical role in organismal health, necessitating sensitive and selective 
      detection methods for quality control and clinical diagnostics. This study 
      demonstrates a dual-modal lateral flow immunoassay for VB(2) quantification using 
      engineered rabbit monoclonal antibodies and core-gap-shell gold nanoparticles in 
      a controlled laboratory setting with processed serum (centrifugation, dilution, 
      filtration). The assay provides two detection modes: a colorimetric mode (visual 
      LOD of 10 ng/mL, instrument-free screening) and a SERS mode (LOD of 4.8 pg/mL, 
      requiring a portable Raman spectrometer). The colorimetric mode represents a 
      20-fold visual improvement and 33-fold instrument-based improvement over 
      conventional AuNPs-LFIA; the SERS mode spans six orders of magnitude 
      (0.001-5000 ng/mL). All reported concentrations are back-calculated to neat 
      serum. The assay demonstrates good specificity and stability, validated by 
      analysis of 30 human serum samples with high concordance to LC-MS. Future 
      integration of plasma separation membranes and a smartphone-based reader is 
      envisioned for true point-of-care use. This work provides a versatile 
      laboratory-based platform for small-molecule biomarker detection and a clear 
      roadmap toward minimal-workflow deployment.
