VI  - 50
DP  - 2026 Jul
TI  - LC-MS/MS of PEth in whole blood: implementation in routine clinical chemistry.
PG  - e00533
LID - 10.1016/j.plabm.2026.e00533 [doi]
LID - e00533
AB  - In clinical practice, assessing alcohol intake can be challenging because 
      patients may under-report or misjudge their consumption, so biomarkers of chronic 
      intake provide valuable additional information. Phosphatidylethanol (PEth) is a 
      group of abnormal phospholipids that are formed in cell membranes only in the 
      presence of ethanol (alcohol). We developed, analytically validated, and 
      clinically implemented a rapid and robust LC-MS/MS-based method for quantifying 
      PEth (16:0/18:1) in whole blood. The method demonstrates a limit of detection 
      (LOD) of 0.01 muM and a limit of quantification (LOQ) of 0.03 muM, with confirmed 
      linearity up to at least 0.8 muM. Validation using reference materials and samples 
      from patients with liver disease and self-reported alcohol intake confirmed its 
      accuracy. Clinical implementation over a two-month period, involving 20 
      analytical batches, demonstrated inter-assay precision below 15%. Our results 
      support the clinical applicability of PEth use for accurate estimation of alcohol 
      intake in humans.
