TI  - A PtRu nanozyme and aggregation-induced emission nanoparticle mediated novel 
      fluorescent and colorimetric dual-mode immunoassay for xylazine detection.
PG  - 149414
LID - S0308-8146(26)01572-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.foodchem.2026.149414 [doi]
AB  - Xylazine (XYL) is an alpha(2) receptor agonist for veterinary use and has abused as 
      an illicit adulterant of opioids. Its abuse poses serious risks including food 
      safety events, drug-facilitated crimes, and even death. Therefore, it is 
      necessary to establish a sensitive, accurate, and rapid method for on-site 
      screening of XYL. In this work, we synthesized a bimetallic PtRu nanoclusters 
      (NCs) nanozyme with high peroxidase-like activity, and an aggregation-induced 
      emission polystyrene nanoparticles (AIE-PSNPs) with strong fluorescence emission 
      at 590 nm. The PtRu NCs catalyze the oxidation of substrate 
      3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), generating a colorimetric signal. Then 
      resulting oxidized TMB quenches the fluorescence of the AIE-PSNPs by inner filter 
      effect, providing a fluorescent signal output. Based on this mechanism, a novel 
      fluorescent and colorimetric dual-mode nanozyme-linked immunosorbent assay 
      (NLISA) was fabricated for sensitive and accurate sensing XYL. The limit of 
      detection of the proposed dual-mode NLISA in fluorescent mode was 
      0.011 ng mL(-1), and in colorimetric mode was 0.049 ng mL(-1), with high 
      specificity and accuracy demonstrated in both modes.
