TI  - Phenylalanine oligopeptide composite magnetic beads conjugated with streptavidin 
      for enhanced specific adsorption as a chemiluminescence immunoassay vector.
PG  - 118657
LID - S0956-5663(26)00289-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2026.118657 [doi]
AB  - Magnetic composite beads hold considerable potential as solid-phase carriers for 
      high-throughput detection in the biomedical field. However, synthesizing 
      composite magnetic beads that exhibit high adsorption capacity and enable 
      efficient immunoassays remains a significant challenge. In this work, we 
      functionalized composite magnetic beads with three phenylalanine oligopeptides 
      (di-, tri-, and tetrapeptides) and investigated the fundamental behavior of short 
      peptide immobilization on the magnetic beads using streptavidin (SA). At a 
      carboxyl density of only 1.5 mmol/g, the phenylalanine dipeptide-grafted beads 
      (PFG@F-2) achieved a high SA adsorption capacity of 62.02 mug/mg. Density 
      functional theory (DFT) calculations revealed that the EDC/NHSS-activated 
      phenylalanine dipeptide exhibited the smallest highest occupied molecular orbital 
      (HOMO) to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy gap alongside the 
      largest LUMO energy, suggesting the mechanism by which PFG@F-2 beads enhance 
      protein adsorption. Chemiluminescence immunoassay (CLIA) demonstrated that the 
      complex formed by the biotinylated CK-MB antibody and SA-conjugated PFG@F-2 
      exhibited a low limit of detection (LOD) of 0.71 pg/mL. The analytical 
      performance of the proposed CLIA was rigorously evaluated. Finally, the practical 
      utility of PFG@F-2 for CK-MB determination in clinical samples was investigated. 
      A strong linear correlation was observed, with a correlation coefficient (r) of 
      0.9948. This study provides valuable insights for the design of functionalized 
      composite magnetic beads with excellent adsorption capacity and enhanced 
      chemiluminescence detection performance.
