TI  - Fluorescence polarization immunoassay based on carbon-based nanomaterials for 
      rapid and highly sensitive detection of imidacloprid in environmental and 
      agricultural samples.
PG  - 129643
LID - S0039-9140(26)00299-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.talanta.2026.129643 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The long-term exposure and easy diffusion of imidacloprid (IMI) pose 
      potential risks to human health and pollinating insects. The sensitivity of 
      conventional fluorescence polarization immunoassay (FPIA) needs enhancement for 
      monitoring trace pesticide residues. RESULTS: Herein, we developed a novel FPIA 
      by employing carbon nanospheres (CNSs) as antibody carriers for fast and highly 
      sensitive detection of IMI. The CNSs-based assay demonstrated superior 
      performance over those using carbon nanotubes (CNTs) or graphene oxide (GO), 
      achieving a detection limit (LOD) of 0.25 mug/L. This represents a 43-fold 
      improvement compared to traditional mAb-based FPIA. Mechanism study revealed that 
      the quasi-spherical structure of CNSs minimizes rotational diffusion anisotropy, 
      leading to the most significant fluorescence polarization change upon binding 
      events, which is the key to sensitivity enhancement. The CNSs-based FPIA 
      exhibited high specificity and was successfully applied to the detection of IMI 
      in pond water, cabbage, apple, corn, and wheat. Spiked recoveries ranged from 
      93.69% to 105.23% with relative standard deviations below 9.57%, indicating that 
      CNSs-based FPIA exhibited good accuracy, precision, and practicality. 
      CONCLUSIONS: This CNSs-based FPIA protocol provides a new avenue for rapid and 
      highly sensitive detection of IMI and other pesticide residues in food safety 
      detection.
