TI  - Electric field-accelerated allosteric DNA-driven immune reaction for highly rapid 
      and sensitive electrochemiluminescence detection of cMyBP-C protein.
PG  - 118542
LID - S0956-5663(26)00174-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2026.118542 [doi]
AB  - An ultra-fast and sensitive electrochemiluminescence (ECL) biosensor was 
      constructed for highly specific detection of acute myocardial infarction 
      associated cardiac myosin-binding protein C (cMyBP-C) via the allosteric DNA 
      regulated immune reaction under electric field effect. Compared with traditional 
      sandwich immunoassays with low specificity, the allosteric DNA-based immunoassay 
      system avoided immune cross-reactivity, in which the specific binding of target 
      protein to two antibodies triggered a conformational change of DNA at a remote 
      site. Moreover, the target recognition efficiency could be enhanced by the 
      modulation of thermodynamic stability in aid of the adjustment of the loop spacer 
      regions length. Meanwhile, the immunoreaction time of the allosteric DNA system 
      was significantly reduced from 1 h to 2 min by the acceleration of the electric 
      field, enhancing diagnostic efficiency of acute myocardial infarction. The 
      sensing platform accomplished accurate and sensitive detection of cMyBP-C with a 
      broad dynamic range spanning from 100 fg/mL to 10 ng/mL and a low limit of 
      detection (LOD) of 28.9 fg/mL. The ECL biosensor was also applied to analyze 80 
      human actual plasma samples, exhibiting superior clinical performance containing 
      exceptional sensitivity, accuracy, and credibility. Thus, the novel strategy for 
      protein detection displayed promising potential in clinical diagnostics.
